<code>assert \result != null && \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < lb.remaining(); \result.words[i] == lb.get(i);</code>
--------------------
<code>assert \result != null && \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < lb.remaining(); \result.words[i] == lb.get(i);</code>

However, since `lb.get(i)` would advance the position of the `LongBuffer`, we need to adjust the assertion to correctly compare the elements without modifying the buffer. We can achieve this by using `\old(lb.get(i))` to capture the original values:

<code>assert \result != null && \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < lb.remaining(); \result.words[i] == \old(lb.get(lb.position() + i));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert \result != null && \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < n; \result.words[i] == lb.get(i);</code>

Explanation:
- `\result != null` ensures that the returned BitSet is not null.
- `\forall int i; 0 <= i && i < n; \result.words[i] == lb.get(i)` ensures that each element in the long array representation of the returned BitSet matches the corresponding element in the LongBuffer from its current position to its limit. Here, `n` is the number of non-zero elements from the position to the limit of the LongBuffer.
--------------------
